\subsection{Scientific and Medical visualization tools}

Frameworks for scientific and medical visualization are needed. VTK (Visual
Toolkit) \cite{VTK}, developed by Kitware, is a good base for developing such
frameworks.

For scientific visualization, ParaView \cite{Paraview} developed by Kitware is
an open-source application built on top of VTK. It is widely used by researchers
and engineers to visualize and analyze large scientific datasets. It provides
many tools for data processing and rendering and can scale to high-performance
computing environments.

For medical visualization, 3D Slicer \cite{Slicer} is an open-source platform
dedicated to medical image visualization and analysis. It provides tools for
image segmentation, 3D reconstruction, registration, and quantitative analysis.
It is widely used in both medical research and clinical applications. A good
amount of the contributions come from Kitware.

\subsection{trame}

\subsubsection{Motivations}

The problems with VTK and 3D Slicer is that they have a steep learning curve,
require C++ skills and can only be run on computers that can run the binaries.

For interoperability, Web solutions also come into mind and, when today most
engineers and researchers know Python, they don't necessarily know web
development or C++ well enough. A study conducted to understand researchers'
habits \cite{researchers_habits} confirms that Python is used by 67\% of
researchers (\cref{fig:researchers_habits_python}).

\insertfigure {assets/images/researchers-python} {width=\textwidth} {from
\cite{researchers_habits}} {fig:researchers_habits_python}

Plus, researchers use open science a lot, with open-source software being more
adopted than commercial solutions, as shown in
\cref{fig:researchers_habits_open_source}.

\insertfigure {assets/images/researchers-open-source} {height=0.3\textheight}
{from \cite{researchers_habits}} {fig:researchers_habits_open_source}

Trame \cite{trame} is an open source Python framework that makes it possible to
easily create custom applications with no skills in compilation, C++ or web
development. It perfectly fits the expectations of researchers and engineers.
Even if indeed VTK.js (JS wrapper of VTK) makes it possible to have VTK in web
it still needs knowledge in Javscript, and ParaView doesn't need C++ skills, it
doesn't make web apps that can be served on any device.

Trame works with widgets, each one leveraging either some UI with special Vue
components or some features with the JS/Python interaction. Trame's strength
comes from its ecosystem. For scientific visualization, \refrepo{trame-vtk} is
the widget that makes it possible to use VTK in trame, for medical
visualization,
\definerepo{https://github.com/KitwareMedical/trame-slicer}{trame-slicer} is the
trame widget that makes it possible to use 3D Slicer in trame.

In \refrepo{trame-vtk} and \refrepo{trame-slicer}, remote rendering can be used,
the application is ran on the Python side and streamed to a HTML image and click
events are transfered to Python, this is the job of
\definerepo{https://github.com/Kitware/trame-rca}{trame-rca} (Remote Controlled
Area) they depend on.

Trame's UI is built from \citev{VueJS} components, Vue is a web UI framework
built for making interactive pages with modular custom components. It has a
gentle learning curve compared to React or Angular. If one wants to add a new UI
element in trame, the first step is to build the Vue component and then wrap it
as a trame widget. It can then be easily added to the trame app's interface.
Vuetify \cite{Vuetify} makes a good toolbox of Vue components that cover the
basic needs like checkboxes, sliders, tables, etc... And
\definerepo{https://github.com/Kitware/trame-vuetify}{trame-vuetify} wraps them
in trame to have a nice UI.

\cref{fig:trame-application} shows how avanced application can be thanks to
\refrepo{trame-vtk} and \refrepo{trame-vuetify}.

